That was "Hunt the Wampus."  I don't know if it was inspired by "Hunt
the Wumpus" (it probably was), but it was a very different game.
Hunt the Wumpus involved numbered rooms (no detail in any of the
rooms;  yopu were just "in" a particular room or not), usually layed
out as the veritces of a dodecahedron (although if I remember
correctly, there was a Wumpus II that allowed you to pick any of about
10 configurations, like 1-way lattice and dendrite, or choose a random
configuration).  In two rooms were giant bats, which would swoop you
to a random room if you approached them.  In two rooms were bottomless
pits, which would kill you if you entered the rooms.  In one room was
the Wumpus, asleep.  The Wumpus would wake up if you shot off an arrow
or bumped him from behind, and start to wander randomly.  If he ended
up in the same room as you after waking up, he would eat you.  You
would win if you shot him--you had 20 arrows.

I'm pretty sure that Hunt the Wumpus was the direct inspiration for
the Wumpus in NetHack.
